    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Universal health coverage and the Sustainable Development Goals: a common challenge for all countries",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-universal-health-coverage-and-the-sustainable-de.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Development Goals: a common challenge for all \ncountries\nA health workforce (HWF) of adequate size and skill mix, as well as required teachers and \ntrainers, are critical to the attainment of any population health goal. This includes the achieve-\nment of universal health coverage (UHC) (WHO 2017c) and the health-related targets of the \nUnited Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). Yet countries globally are affected by \nhealth workforce challenges of a multifaceted nature, such as difficulties in the education and \ntraining, deployment, performance and retention of their health workforc",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains why a health workforce of adequate size, skill mix, and distribution is essential for achieving universal health coverage (UHC) and the health-related Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), and why comprehensive workforce data are needed to address shortages and labour market imbalances.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The Global Strategy on Human Resources for Health",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-the-global-strategy-on-human-resources-for-healt.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The Global Strategy on Human Resources for Health: Workforce 2030 (WHO 2016c), adopted by \nthe Sixty-ninth World Health Assembly in May 2016 under resolution WHA69.19, aims to ensure \nuniversal accessibility, acceptability, coverage and quality of health workforces within strength-\nened health systems(World Health Assembly 2016). This can be achieved through adequate \ninvestments and the implementation of effective policies at national, regional and global levels. \nTo realize this, the GSHRH presents multiple objectives including strengthening HWF data, \nevidence and knowledge. Translated into",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarizes the Global Strategy on Human Resources for Health: Workforce 2030 (adopted under resolution WHA69.19), highlighting its four objectives, global milestones for 2020 and 2030, and call for progressive implementation of National Health Workforce Accounts.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The Action Plan of the High-Level Commission on Health Employment and Economic Growth",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-the-action-plan-of-the-high-level-commission-on.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The Action Plan of the High-Level Commission on \nHealth Employment and Economic Growth \nIn line with the GSHRH, the High-Level Commission on Health Employment and Economic \nGrowth published ‘Working for Health and Growth’ (High-Level Commission on Health Employ-\nment and Economic 2016), a report and five-year action plan for health employment and \ninclusive economic growth. The three agencies that support the action plan are the Interna-\ntional Labour Organization (ILO), the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development \n(OECD) and WHO. This report was reviewed by the Seventieth World",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reviews the report and five-year action plan of the UN High-Level Commission on Health Employment and Economic Growth (supported by ILO, OECD, and WHO), noting its ten recommendations on job creation, gender equality, education, and international migration and the role of NHWA in monitoring them.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Benefits of the National Health Workforce Accounts",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-benefits-of-the-national-health-workforce-accoun.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "empty"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Benefits at the global level",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-benefits-at-the-global-level.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "From the Action Plan of the High-Level Commission, and specifically the GSHRH, the concept \nof NHWA is clearly promoted as an important means to support countries in their national HWF \npolicy and planning. The Sixty-ninth World Health Assembly adopted resolution WHA69.19 in \nsupport of its implementation(World Health Assembly 2016). The purpose of the NHWA is to \nfacilitate the standardization of health workforce information systems for interoperability, i.e. \nthe ability to exchange health workforce data within broader subnational or national health \ninformation systems, as well as within in",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Outlines the global-level benefits of the NHWA in standardizing health workforce information systems for interoperability, supporting monitoring of the GSHRH and SDG targets, and streamlining international data reporting across WHO, OECD, and Eurostat.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Benefits at the national level",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-benefits-at-the-national-level.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The NHWA indicators have been selected with clear policy relevance in mind, so that they \ncan be used by any country according to its own specific needs. While designed to support a \ncoherent framework for HWF policy analysis and design, the NHWA can be implemented and \nused in a flexible, modular way. It is important for countries to note that while the 10 NHWA \nmodules provide full coverage of indicators for all components and policy domains and form \na natural whole, there is also significant room for selecting country-specific indicators. As \ncountries strongly differ in terms of the build",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the national-level benefits of implementing the NHWA as a flexible, modular framework tailored to country policy priorities, accompanied by five guiding principles on country ownership, multisectoral governance, progressive strengthening of HRHIS, data security, and alignment with existing systems.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Benefits at the regional level",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-benefits-at-the-regional-level.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Apart from the benefits at national level, the NHWA can also ease the comparability of the HWF \nlandscape at regional level and globally. Through country reporting and international compar-\nisons, the NHWA may facilitate cross-country capacity-building, information and knowledge \nexchange, as well as more sophisticated research about future trends of health workforces \nwithin and across systems.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Highlights the regional-level benefits of the NHWA in improving cross-country comparability of health workforce data, tracking bilateral and multilateral mobility, and supporting regional observatories and economic integration agreements.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
