    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The underpinning framework",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-the-underpinning-framework.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The concept of the NHWA is closely aligned with the health labour market framework for UHC \n(Sousa et al. 2013) (Figure 1), which also takes a central place in the High-Level Commission’s \nreport ‘Working for health and growth: investing in the health workforce’(High-Level Commis-\nsion on Health Employment and Economic 2016). This framework provides a comprehensive \npicture of the education sector and health labour market dynamics, in which the economy, \nNational Health Workforce Accounts: A Handbook\npopulation and society act as drivers to attaining UHC and optimal health-care services as the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents the health labour market framework underpinning the NHWA (Figure 1), showing how education pipelines and labour market dynamics interact under four core policy domains—production, inflows and outflows, maldistribution and inefficiencies, and private-sector regulation—to achieve universal health coverage.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "A modular approach",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-a-modular-approach.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A modular approach\nThe NHWA contains a set of 78 core indicators, spread over 10 modules, that aim to support \nnational-level HWF policies to progress towards UHC and SDGs. The indicators in the 10 \nmodules feed into three crucial labour market components: the education component, the \nlabour force component and the component serving population health needs (Figure 2).\nFigure 2: Overview of labour market components supported by the NHWA \nmodules\nModule 1: Active health workforce stock\nThis module provides a comprehensive overview of the composition and distribution of \nthe HWF. Indicators are",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the modular architecture of the NHWA (Figure 2), organizing 78 core indicators across 10 modules that span health workforce education (Modules 2–4), the active labour force (Modules 1 and 5–7), and serving population health needs (Modules 8–10).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Implementation of the NHWA",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-implementation-of-the-nhwa.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The NHWA contains indicators with clear policy relevance across the entire spectrum of HWF \npriorities and those related to the health labour market framework. The implementation of the \nNHWA is a country-led activity, building on national systems and using existing mechanisms to \ncoordinate data-gathering for the evaluation of the health workforce.\nWHO has developed a set of tools to help countries implement the NHWA. These include, in \naddition to the Handbook, an implementation guide and a web platform for data reporting, visu-\nalization, and conducting international comparisons.\nThe WHO re",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Emphasizes that NHWA implementation is a country-led, intersectoral process building on existing national and subnational health workforce databases, supported by the WHO NHWA online platform, implementation guide, and regional training workshops.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Potential use of the NHWA",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-potential-use-of-the-nhwa.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "As described in the metadata sheets, the NHWA includes both numeric and ‘capability’ indica-\ntors that provide information on regulation and other mechanisms related to the health work-\nforce. Tangible examples on what implementation of the NHWA can mean for countries, and \nhow it can support them in policy-making at national level, are provided in Annex 3.\nIt is implicit that the availability of the institutional processes, plans and units listed in \nModule 9: Governance and health workforce policies maximizes in each case the use of the \nindicators in a policy relevant direction. In addition",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that the NHWA combines quantitative numeric indicators and qualitative capability indicators to support national policy-making, pointing to worked policy applications in Annex 3.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789241513111-eng/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Methodological note on implementation",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-methodological-note-on-implementation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Annual measurement\nThe monitoring of indicators in the NHWA is expected to occur on annual basis, although more \nregular monitoring schemes are possible. This implies reporting on indicators to cover a period \nof one year. Where data reporting is expected less regularly, this is marked in the individual \nindicator descriptive sheets.\nPart I: National Health Workforce Accounts in context\n13\nMeasurements may be performed using three different time frames. \nCross-sectional measurement\nSuch measurement provides a snapshot of a metric for a specific year, as at the end of the \nyear. For example, th",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Provides methodological guidance for implementing NHWA indicators, covering annual measurement periods, public/private sector and subnational disaggregation, health workforce occupation classifications (ISCO-08) and activity status categories (practising, professionally active, licensed), and major national data sources.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
