      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Example 2: Balanced geographical distribution of the health workforce",
      "pageStart": 144,
      "pageEnd": 146,
      "target": "../sections/sec-110-example-2-balanced-geographical-distribution-of.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "health workforce\nMany countries suffer from geographical imbalances in the distribution of their health work-\nforce, with most (severe) shortages arising in rural and remote areas. Establishing an evalua-\ntion of this maldistribution is a starter to evaluate further the influence of social, financial and \norganizational determinants on health worker decisions to be a candidate for, stay in, or leave a \njob in underserved areas – and hence to increase population access to health workers in these \nareas. Monitoring geographical distribution supports the Global Strategy on Human Resources \nfor He",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents Example 2 in Annex 3 on achieving a balanced geographical distribution of the health workforce, mapping indicators from Modules 1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, and 10 to evaluate rural retention, compulsory service, and subnational service coverage.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
